      { number: 1, title: 'Bricks, Beads and Bones: The Harappan Civilisation', priority: 'Must Read', upscRelevance: 'High', upscRelevanceNote: 'Subsistence strategies (Grains, Animal bones); Agricultural technologies (Bull terracotta, Ploughed field at Kalibangan, Canals at Shortughai, Water reservoirs at Dholavira); Mohenjodaro Planned Urban Centre: Citadel vs Lower Town, Drainage system (covered drains), Great Bath (bitumen waterproofing); Social differences tracking (Burials grave goods, Luxury faience pots vs utilitarian chert blades); Craft production at Chanhudaro (Carnelian, Steatite, Micro-beads, Specialized drills); Trade networks (Dilmun Bahrain, Magan Oman, Meluhha Harappa, Lapis Lazuli at Shortughai); Seals, Script (Undeciphered, Boustrophedon right-to-left), Weights (Chert binary 1,2,4,8,16... and decimal); Ancient Authority theories (Single ruler vs Multiple rulers vs No ruler); Decline of Harappan civilization (Climatic change, Deforestation, Aryan invasion hypothesis critique by George Dales).', keyThemes: ['Harappan urban architecture & sanitation', 'Craft specialization & long-distance trade', 'Weights & measures standardization', 'Decline theories analysis'], hasResources: true },
      { number: 2, title: 'Kings, Farmers and Towns: Early States and Economies (c. 600 BCE - 600 CE)', priority: 'Must Read', upscRelevance: 'High', upscRelevanceNote: 'Decipherment of Brahmi and Kharosthi by James Prinsep (1838: Piyadassi identification with Ashoka); The Sixteen Mahajanapadas (Magadha, Vajji, Gandhara, Kosala, Kashi); Why Magadha became pre-eminent? (Iron mines, Fertile agriculture, Elephants in army, Ganga/Son trade, Strategic capitals Rajagriha & Pataliputra); The Mauryan Empire: Sources (Megasthenes\' Indica, Kautilya\'s Arthashastra, Buddhist/Jaina texts, Ashokan Rock Edicts), Administration (5 major political centres: Pataliputra, Taxila, Ujjayini, Tosali, Suvarnagiri, 6 sub-committees for military), Ashoka\'s Dhamma principles; New Notions of Kingship: Kushanas and divine kingship (Devaputra title, Mat sculpture), Satavahanas (Matronymics, Land grants to Brahmanas), Guptas (Prashastis: Harishena\'s Prayag Prashasti for Samudragupta); Changing Countryside: Agrarian expansion (Iron-tipped ploughshares, Transplantation of paddy, Irrigation tanks/canals), Social differences (Gahapati, Vellalar, Uzhavar, Adimai), Land grants (Prabhavati Gupta land grant inscription); Towns and Trade: Urban centres, Punch-marked coins (Silver/copper), Kushana gold coins (highest purity), Gupta gold coins (Dinar), Roman trade coins.', keyThemes: ['Ashokan epigraphy & Dhamma policy', 'Mauryan administrative architecture', 'Kushana & Gupta divine kingship models', 'Coinage evolution in ancient India'], hasResources: true },
      { number: 3, title: 'Kinship, Caste and Class: Early Societies (c. 600 BCE - 600 CE)', priority: 'Must Read', upscRelevance: 'High', upscRelevanceNote: 'The Critical Edition of the Mahabharata: V.S. Sukthankar team project (1919-1966, 13,000 pages collation across Sanskrit manuscripts, Regional variations vs Common elements); Kinship and Marriage: Patriliny system, Rules of marriage (Endogamy, Exogamy, Polygyny, Polyandry), Gotra rules (Gotra of woman changes after marriage, Satavahana exception: Gotra retention and polygyny); The Caste System: Dharmasutras and Dharmashastras ideal social order, 4 Varnas (Brahmana, Kshatriya, Vaishya, Shudra), Non-Kshatriya rulers (Shungas/Kanvas Brahmanas, Shakas Mlechchhas, Satavahanas Ekabrahmana), Jatis and occupational guilds (Mandasor silk weavers inscription guild); Beyond the Four Varnas: Nishadas (Ekalavya), Mlechchhas (Forest dwellers), Untouchables / Chandalas (Manusmriti duties: using discarded utensils, executioner duties; Chinese pilgrims Faxian and Xuan Zang accounts on untouchability); Beyond Birth: Gendered access to property (Stridhana, Manusmriti patriarchal property restriction, Prabhavati Gupta royal exception), Varna and access to property (Brahmanical justification vs Buddhist critique of social inequality: Sutta Pitaka story of Mahasammata social contract).', keyThemes: ['Mahabharata Critical Edition methodology', 'Gotra exogamy & Satavahana matrimonial exceptions', 'Manusmriti social stratification & Chandalas', 'Early Buddhist social contract theory'], hasResources: true },
      { number: 4, title: 'Thinkers, Beliefs and Buildings: Cultural Developments (c. 600 BCE - 600 CE)', priority: 'Must Read', upscRelevance: 'High', upscRelevanceNote: 'A Glimpse of Sanchi: Why Sanchi Stupa survived while Amaravati Stupa fell to ruins? (French and British museums desire averted by Shahjehan Begum and Sultan Jehan Begum plaster casts and funding for guest house/museum); The Background: Sacrificial traditions (Rigvedic sacrifices, Rajasuya, Ashvamedha), Upanishadic philosophical debates (Kutasagarashala debates, Soul/Atman and Brahman); Beyond Worldly Pleasures: The Message of Mahavira (Jainism philosophy: Entire world animated including stones/water, Ahimsa non-injury, Karma cycle, Asceticism and penance, Five Great Vows: Non-violence, Truth, Non-stealing, Celibacy, Non-possession); The Buddha and the Quest for Enlightenment: Life of Siddhartha Gautama, Four Noble Truths, Eightfold Path (Ashtanga Marga), Impermanence of world (Anicca), No permanent soul (Anatta), Middle Path; Followers of the Buddha: The Sangha (Democratic functioning, Monks/Bhikkus, Entry of women: Mahapajapati Gotami first Bhikkhuni, Therigatha verses), The Buddhist Canon / Tipitaka (Vinaya Pitaka: Sangha rules, Sutta Pitaka: Buddha\'s teachings, Abhidhamma Pitaka: Philosophical matters); Stupas: Why were Stupas built? (Relics of Buddha enshrined, Chaityas), Structure of Stupa (Anda hemispherical mound, Harmika balcony representing abode of gods, Yashti mast, Chhatri umbrella, Pradakshina patha stone railing, Torana carved gateways); Sculpture at Sanchi: Stories in stone (Vessantara Jataka), Symbols of worship (Empty throne meditation, Stupa Mahaparinibbana, Wheel Dharmachakrapravartana), Popular traditions (Shalabhanjika auspicious bracket figure, Gaja-Lakshmi, Serpents); New Religious Traditions: Mahayana Buddhism (Buddha as God, Bodhisattva savior concept, Image worship) vs Hinayana / Theravada; Growth of Puranic Hinduism: Vaishnavism (10 Avataras), Shaivism (Lingam and human forms), Temple architecture evolution (Small square room Garbhagriha, Shikhara tower over shrine, Mandapa assembly hall, Carved gateway Gopuram, Monolithic temples e.g. Kailashnath at Ellora).', keyThemes: ['Sanchi vs Amaravati Stupa conservation history', 'Jainism Five Vows & Ahimsa ethics', 'Tipitaka classification (Vinaya, Sutta, Abhidhamma)', 'Stupa architectural elements (Anda, Harmika, Torana)', 'Mahayana vs Hinayana doctrinal divergence', 'Early temple architecture progression (Garbhagriha, Shikhara)'], hasResources: true }
